on of  Woodrow Wilson or Calvin Coolidge when  she was wishing the Coolidge is goodbye  it was a big symbol of the difference  between the promise of America and the  reality because here you have a  beautiful monuments in Washington which  was and supposed to be sort of the  earthly example of what America could be  and at the same time black people had to  sit in different parts of movie theaters  and the same things with restaurants  until very late in the 20th century  public schools and absolutely public  schools and you know for younger people  especially they see this is so  inevitable they can't believe that  within the lifetimes of many people now  living that people went through this the  other thing on a lighter note Benton  been mentioned LBJ showing his  gallbladder scar and Kay Graham had this  long very complex relationship with  Lyndon Johnson which foundered when she  began to be skeptical of the war in  Vietnam when LBJ showed his gallbladder  scar it was said that many people were  grateful that he had not had hemorrhoid  surgery  yes sir came here during Coolidge to be  near my mother so and if you have a lot  of individual memories much of which  you're touched on in the book but I  wonder if the top of the head if the  panelists would just say what right off  the top here and what is one's very  strong memory you have whether it's the  Kennedy assassination or whatever it is  well the question is for the panel  what's one very strong memory you have  of this city EV well I'll go with my  most recent strong memory of this city  and that comes from this afternoon when  Bob and I were wandering around I  decided that as a New Yorker he needed  to see some more of this city and not  just in the page of the book so we went  to the Library of Congress and then we  went to the Folger and we were sitting  in sort of a little alcove off to the  side and just started talking and we  were talking and talking and finally I  said maybe we should it was about ten  after four quarter after four I said  well you know let's go and take a look  at the theater and then we'll go out  well turn out the Folger closed at four  o'clock so we were very nearly trapped  in the Folger wondering how we were  gonna get out of there but but I think  as a memory of the city I think that  that anything that the city is very real  to me as well and and accessible and but  that's one that I will remember all  three  you don't me are you indeed you are with  me right now as always I Michael  reminded me when he was talking about  what a segregated City it was that I'm  an army brat and I lived at Fort Myer  and Virginia Frome I we had my father  headquarters at Fort Myer we lived on  the parade ground and I when Martin  Luther King came to town and there was  this huge gathering on the mall my my  step-grandmother not my favorite she was  the wicked step grandmother from  Savannah Georgia called and started  screaming at us to get out of town  because those African bullies were  invading the city and we had to leave  this horrible city also we were there  when Jack Kennedy was assassinated so I  do remember my brother and sister and I  walking down to the edge of Arlington  Cemetery and watching is what we could  see at the funeral from right in front  of our house because we lived right  there next to the chapel but one memory  is when we were at a party when you  mentioned the library the Library of  Congress one night there was some sort  of event been and Michael Beschloss and  I were there and Michael and I walked  over to the bar to get a drink and we  looked out it was sunset and we looked  out across Washington and there was the  Washington Monument we were you know the  Capitol the Lincoln Memorial we could  see the whole city and as the Sun was  setting on this city I just almost burst  into tears and I grabbed Michael I said  look at this city is this not the most  beautiful city in the whole world who  couldn't love this city do